{
  "assessment": "The source provides a quantitative test of Kramers-Langer theory for thermally activated inversion of a five-ion pyramidal Coulomb cluster. The identical-ion cluster (40Ca+) exhibits a low-barrier pseudo-rotation pathway with measured inversion rates spanning two orders of magnitude, fitted to K-L theory with a fitted temperature T=1.8±0.1 mK and a background collision rate R_bg=0.10±0.03 s^-1. The isotope-substituted cluster (44Ca+ at apex) shows suppression of inversions via a high-barrier turnstile rotation with barrier ~270 mK. The source provides two quantitative regimes (identical vs. isotope-substituted) sharing the control alpha, but the isotope-substituted branch lacks a measured rate formula; only a qualitative suppression is reported. The identical-ion branch provides a full quantitative rate formula (K-L theory) with fitted parameters. Thus, the extraction focuses on the identical-ion cluster's inversion rate as a function of alpha, with the isotope-substituted branch as a qualitative contrast. The source supports a numeric control (alpha), a numeric readout (inversion rate), a quantitative mechanism (K-L rate equation), and an objective response within an explicit domain, so case_authoring_ready is true.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Akhil Ayyadevara, Symmetry-Controlled Thermal Activation in Pyramidal Coulomb Clusters: Testing Kramers-Langer Theory, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/xsdn-srjd",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/xsdn-srjd",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2601.04883v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper experimentally and numerically studies thermally activated inversion of a five-ion square-pyramidal Coulomb cluster in a Paul trap. It tests multidimensional Kramers-Langer theory for identical 40Ca+ ions, where inversion proceeds via a low-barrier pseudo-rotation, and compares with isotope-substituted 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 clusters, where inversion is suppressed via a high-barrier turnstile rotation. The main quantitative result is the measured inversion rate as a function of trap aspect ratio alpha, fitted to K-L theory to extract cluster temperature and background collision rate.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "kramers_langer_rate",
      "statement": "The escape rate from a local minimum predicted by multidimensional Kramers-Langer theory is kappa = N * (lambda^+ / 2pi) * (det U / |det U'|)^(1/2) * exp(-E_b / k_B T), where E_b is the energy barrier, U and U' are Hessians at equilibrium and saddle, lambda^+ is the single positive eigenvalue of the dynamical matrix at the saddle, and N is the number of equivalent pathways.",
      "equations": [
        "kappa = N * (lambda^+ / 2pi) * (det U / |det U'|)^(1/2) * exp(-E_b / k_B T)",
        "det(m lambda^2 I + gamma lambda I + U') = 0"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["kappa", "N", "lambda^+", "U", "U'", "E_b", "k_B", "T", "gamma"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "N_pathways_identical",
          "value": 4,
          "uncertainty": "none",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Multiplicity factor for identical-ion cluster; apex can replace any of four base ions.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 147,
              "line_end": 147,
              "support": "We also set N = 4 since the apex ion can replace any of the four base ions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "T_fitted_cluster",
          "value": 0.0018,
          "uncertainty": "0.0001 K (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "K",
          "role": "Fitted cluster temperature from experimental inversion rates using K-L theory.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 151,
              "line_end": 151,
              "support": "The excess rate for alpha < alpha_th follows the K-L prediction over two orders of magnitude for a fitted temperature of T = 1.8 ± 0.1 mK."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "R_bg_background_collisions",
          "value": 0.1,
          "uncertainty": "0.03 s^-1 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "s^-1",
          "role": "Saturated inversion rate due to background-gas collisions for alpha > alpha_th.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 151,
              "line_end": 151,
              "support": "For alpha > alpha_th, it saturates to R_bg = 0.10 ± 0.03 s^-1 due to background-gas collisions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "E_b_turnstile_isotope",
          "value": 0.27,
          "uncertainty": "none",
          "unit": "K",
          "role": "Activation barrier for turnstile rotation in isotope-substituted cluster (44Ca+ at apex).",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 450,
              "line_end": 450,
              "support": "The activation barrier is ~270 mK, which is significantly lower than the umbrella inversion but sufficiently high to suppress thermal activation at experimental temperatures."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 137,
          "line_end": 145,
          "support": "The escape rate from a local minimum predicted by the multidimensional K-L theory is kappa = N (lambda^+/2pi) (det U / |det U'|)^(1/2) exp(-E_b/k_B T), where E_b is the energy barrier and U, U' are Hessians at equilibrium and saddle. The prefactor lambda^+ is the single positive eigenvalue of the dynamical matrix at the saddle, det(m lambda^2 I + gamma lambda I + U') = 0. The factor N denotes the number of equivalent pathways."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "identical_ions_40Ca",
      "statement": "Cluster consists of five identical 40Ca+ ions.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 79,
          "line_end": 79,
          "support": "We experimentally realize the inversion dynamics of a square-pyramidal cluster of five 40Ca+ ions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "isotope_substituted_44Ca_apex",
      "statement": "Cluster is 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 with the heavier isotope at the apex.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 109,
          "line_end": 109,
          "support": "We break the permutation symmetry by substituting one ion with a heavier isotope (44Ca+), which preferentially occupies the apex of the pyramid."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "alpha_identical_experiment",
      "statement": "Trap aspect ratio alpha ≈ 1.09 for the identical-ion cluster in the isotope comparison.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ≈ 1.09), the (40Ca+)5 cluster undergoes rapid inversions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "alpha_isotope_experiment",
      "statement": "Trap aspect ratio alpha ≈ 1.09 for the isotope-substituted cluster in the isotope comparison.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ≈ 1.09), the 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 cluster remains locked in one orientation over long periods."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "cooling_laser_parameters",
      "statement": "Doppler cooling with 397 nm cooling beam red detuned by ~3 Gamma and repump at 866 nm; photon scattering rate Gamma/2pi ≈ 22 MHz; secular frequencies ~1 MHz.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 126,
          "line_end": 126,
          "support": "The photon scattering rate (Gamma/2pi ≈ 22 MHz) is much larger than the secular frequencies (omega_x,z/2pi ≈ 1 MHz)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 412,
          "line_end": 412,
          "support": "The cooling laser (4s S1/2 -> 4p P1/2 at 397 nm) and repump laser (3d D3/2 -> 4p P1/2 at 866 nm) beams are locked... The repump beam is locked on the resonance, while the cooling beam is red detuned by ~3 Gamma."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "rf_trap_parameters",
      "statement": "End-cap type 3D Paul trap with rf-drive frequency Omega_rf = 2pi × 18.26 MHz.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 384,
          "line_end": 384,
          "support": "We confine the calcium ions in an end-cap type 3D Paul trap with an rf-drive frequency Omega_rf = 2pi × 18.26 MHz."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "md_simulation_parameters",
      "statement": "MD simulations use Langevin dynamics with friction gamma/m ≈ 6×10^3 s^-1, time step 10^-8 s, 10^9 steps, and temperatures 1.5-3 mK.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 497,
          "line_end": 497,
          "support": "The friction is set to the experimental cooling value (gamma/m ≈ 6×10^3 s^-1)... The trajectories are evolved for 10^9 steps with a time step Delta t = 10^-8 s."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 147,
          "line_end": 147,
          "support": "The rates obtained from MD simulations show excellent parameter-free agreement with the K-L predictions over a wide range of alpha for temperatures of 1.5-3 mK."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "alpha",
      "definition": "Trap aspect ratio alpha = omega_z / omega_x, controlled by superimposing a dc voltage on the trap electrodes.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
      "minimum": 1.05,
      "maximum": 1.29,
      "allowed_values": [],
      "domain_statement": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima of V are two sets of inversion-broken square pyramids.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 90,
          "line_end": 90,
          "support": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima of V are two sets of inversion-broken square pyramids."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "inversion_rate",
      "definition": "Rate of thermally activated inversion between the two pyramidal configurations, extracted from exponential fits to dwell-time histograms of the parity-odd octupole moment psi_30.",
      "unit": "s^-1",
      "statistic": "Exponential fit to dwell-time histogram tail; rate from exponential distribution of dwell times.",
      "uncertainty": "Uncertainty from exponential fit; specific values not given for each alpha.",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 133,
          "line_end": 133,
          "support": "We extract the inversion rate kappa from an exponential distribution of the dwell time tau from more than 2000 trajectories."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 412,
          "line_end": 412,
          "support": "From the psi_30 time series, we bin the dwell times into a histogram and fit an exponential to the tail."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "identical_ion_rate_KL",
      "statement": "For the identical-ion (40Ca+)5 cluster, the experimentally measured inversion rate for alpha < alpha_th (~1.1) follows the Kramers-Langer prediction over two orders of magnitude, with a fitted temperature T = 1.8 ± 0.1 mK and a background collision rate R_bg = 0.10 ± 0.03 s^-1 for alpha > alpha_th.",
      "quantitative_form": "kappa(alpha) = N * (lambda^+/2pi) * (det U / |det U'|)^(1/2) * exp(-E_b(alpha)/k_B T) for alpha < alpha_th; kappa = R_bg for alpha > alpha_th, with T = 1.8e-3 K, R_bg = 0.10 s^-1, N = 4.",
      "mechanism_ids": ["kramers_langer_rate"],
      "control_names": ["alpha"],
      "control_domains": [
        {
          "control_name": "alpha",
          "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
          "minimum": 1.05,
          "maximum": 1.29,
          "allowed_values": [],
          "domain_statement": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima are inversion-broken pyramids; the measured rates span alpha below and above alpha_th ~1.1.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 90,
              "line_end": 90,
              "support": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima of V are two sets of inversion-broken square pyramids."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 151,
              "line_end": 151,
              "support": "For alpha < alpha_th ~1.1, it grows rapidly... whereas for alpha > alpha_th, it saturates to R_bg."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "readout_names": ["inversion_rate"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["identical_ions_40Ca", "cooling_laser_parameters", "rf_trap_parameters"],
      "applicability": "Applies to the identical-ion (40Ca+)5 cluster in the bistable pyramidal regime 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, with Doppler cooling parameters as described. The K-L prediction is parameter-free for MD simulations; the experimental fit uses T and R_bg as fitted parameters.",
      "statistical_basis": "Experimental rates from exponential fits to dwell-time histograms; MD rates from more than 2000 trajectories; agreement over two orders of magnitude.",
      "uncertainty": "Fitted temperature T = 1.8 ± 0.1 mK and background rate R_bg = 0.10 ± 0.03 s^-1; uncertainty convention not specified.",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 151,
          "line_end": 151,
          "support": "The experimentally measured inversion rate exhibits a clear threshold behavior. For alpha < alpha_th ~1.1, it grows rapidly... whereas for alpha > alpha_th, it saturates to R_bg = 0.10 ± 0.03 s^-1. The excess rate for alpha < alpha_th follows the K-L prediction over two orders of magnitude for a fitted temperature of T = 1.8 ± 0.1 mK."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "isotope_substitution_suppression",
      "statement": "For the isotope-substituted cluster 44Ca+(40Ca+)4, the permutation symmetry is broken, closing the low-barrier pseudo-rotation channel; the system must invert via a high-barrier turnstile rotation with activation barrier ~270 mK, resulting in suppression of thermally activated inversions compared to the identical-ion cluster at the same alpha ≈ 1.09.",
      "quantitative_form": "E_b_turnstile ≈ 270 mK; no quantitative rate formula provided; qualitative suppression observed.",
      "mechanism_ids": ["kramers_langer_rate"],
      "control_names": ["alpha"],
      "control_domains": [
        {
          "control_name": "alpha",
          "domain_kind": "fixed_value",
          "minimum": null,
          "maximum": null,
          "allowed_values": [1.09],
          "domain_statement": "The isotope comparison was performed at alpha ≈ 1.09.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 111,
              "line_end": 111,
              "support": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ≈ 1.09), the (40Ca+)5 cluster undergoes rapid inversions, whereas the 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 cluster remains locked in one orientation over long periods."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "readout_names": ["inversion_rate"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["isotope_substituted_44Ca_apex", "alpha_isotope_experiment", "cooling_laser_parameters", "rf_trap_parameters"],
      "applicability": "Applies to the isotope-substituted cluster with 44Ca+ at the apex, at alpha ≈ 1.09, under the same trapping and cooling conditions as the identical-ion experiment.",
      "statistical_basis": "Qualitative observation from fluorescence time traces; no quantitative rate extracted for the isotope-substituted cluster.",
      "uncertainty": "No quantitative uncertainty reported for the suppression; barrier value ~270 mK from cNEB calculation.",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 109,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "The pseudo rotation is no longer viable as it does not invert the pyramid. Instead, the ions have to undergo a cooperative exchange of three ions akin to a turnstile rotation, with much higher energy barriers. Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ≈ 1.09), the (40Ca+)5 cluster undergoes rapid inversions, whereas the 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 cluster remains locked in one orientation over long periods."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 450,
          "line_end": 450,
          "support": "The activation barrier is ~270 mK, which is significantly lower than the umbrella inversion but sufficiently high to suppress thermal activation at experimental temperatures."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_ambiguities": [
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "uncertainty_convention_unreported",
      "statement": "The paper reports uncertainties for fitted temperature (T = 1.8 ± 0.1 mK) and background rate (R_bg = 0.10 ± 0.03 s^-1) without specifying whether they are standard errors, confidence intervals, or another convention.",
      "claim_ids": ["identical_ion_rate_KL"],
      "mechanism_ids": ["kramers_langer_rate"],
      "control_names": [],
      "readout_names": ["inversion_rate"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 151,
          "line_end": 151,
          "support": "The excess rate for alpha < alpha_th follows the K-L prediction over two orders of magnitude for a fitted temperature of T = 1.8 ± 0.1 mK. ... it saturates to R_bg = 0.10 ± 0.03 s^-1."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
